<h1 id="the-problem">The Problem</h1>

<p>The integer array <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nums</code> is sorted in ascending order, with all values being distinct.</p>

<p>Before being passed to the function, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nums</code> was rotated at an unknown pivot index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">k</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">0 &lt;= k &lt; nums.length</code>) such that the array becomes <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[nums[k], nums[k+1], ..., nums[n-1], nums[0], nums[1], ..., nums[k-1]]</code> (0-indexed). For example, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[0,1,2,4,5,6,7]</code> rotated at index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">3</code> might become <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]</code>.</p>

<p>Given the rotated array <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nums</code> and an integer <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">target</code>, return the index of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">target</code> if it exists in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nums</code>, otherwise return <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-1</code>.</p>

<p>You must design an algorithm with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">O(log n)</code> time complexity to solve this problem.</p>

